WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.713
In this lesson you'll learn the difference

2
00:00:03.713 --> 00:00:05.652
between a program on your disk,

3
00:00:05.652 --> 00:00:07.800
a process running it right now,

4
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.785
and a service your computer keeps running

5
00:00:09.785 --> 00:00:12.061
in the background with no window.

6
00:00:12.061 --> 00:00:14.731
A program is a file of instructions.

7
00:00:14.731 --> 00:00:17.366
A process is one running copy of it.

8
00:00:17.366 --> 00:00:19.387
A service is a process the operating

9
00:00:19.387 --> 00:00:22.115
system starts and keeps alive by itself.

10
00:00:22.115 --> 00:00:25.157
File, running copy, kept alive.

11
00:00:25.157 --> 00:00:27.432
"Port already in use",

12
00:00:27.432 --> 00:00:29.580
a server that outlived its window,

13
00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:33.655
an AI tool busy in the background: all one idea.

14
00:00:33.655 --> 00:00:35.838
Your agent can find and stop those.

15
00:00:35.838 --> 00:00:38.427
Your part is knowing what ought to be running.

16
00:00:38.427 --> 00:00:39.902
Aaron, who made this course,

17
00:00:39.902 --> 00:00:42.433
has an Information Systems degree,

18
00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:44.511
and once wrote Python in Notepad,

19
00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:48.458
saved as .txt, with no idea how to run it.

20
00:00:48.458 --> 00:00:49.607
It just sat there:

21
00:00:49.607 --> 00:00:53.034
a program that never became a process.

22
00:00:53.484 --> 00:00:55.678
This lesson sits in the first district,

23
00:00:55.678 --> 00:00:57.536
The Machine, which asks:

24
00:00:57.536 --> 00:00:59.393
what's on your computer?

25
00:00:59.393 --> 00:01:01.762
Module one, lesson six.

26
00:01:01.762 --> 00:01:03.027
Last lesson,

27
00:01:03.027 --> 00:01:05.535
every program left home with a satchel:

28
00:01:05.535 --> 00:01:07.880
a copy of its parent's settings.

29
00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:09.761
Now meet the programs carrying them,

30
00:01:09.761 --> 00:01:11.899
and count how many are out.

31
00:01:12.349 --> 00:01:15.159
Picture a kitchen shelf of recipe cards.

32
00:01:15.159 --> 00:01:16.772
Each card is a program:

33
00:01:16.772 --> 00:01:19.002
instructions, doing nothing.

34
00:01:19.002 --> 00:01:21.730
A process is a cook working from one card,

35
00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.203
right now. It gets a ticket number,

36
00:01:24.203 --> 00:01:27.767
called a process ID, or PID.

37
00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:30.820
Its own stretch of counter, which is memory.

38
00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:32.922
And the satchel handed over at the door

39
00:01:32.922 --> 00:01:34.860
by whoever sent it in:

40
00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:39.075
File Explorer, a terminal, an editor, an agent.

41
00:01:39.075 --> 00:01:41.130
That sender is its parent.

42
00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:44.450
Many cooks can work from the same card at once.

43
00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:46.563
On the laptop this course is made on,

44
00:01:46.563 --> 00:01:48.351
eleven copies of node were running

45
00:01:48.351 --> 00:01:50.197
at one moment this month.

46
00:01:50.197 --> 00:01:53.519
One recipe, eleven processes.

47
00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:57.256
Under the picture, a process is private memory,

48
00:01:57.256 --> 00:02:00.355
a PID, a copy of the environment,

49
00:02:00.355 --> 00:02:04.535
and an account it runs as, usually yours.

50
00:02:04.535 --> 00:02:06.497
Inside are threads:

51
00:02:06.497 --> 00:02:08.169
workers sharing its counter.

52
00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:09.725
That's module seven.

53
00:02:09.725 --> 00:02:12.906
On the course laptop, at one moment, four hundred

54
00:02:12.906 --> 00:02:15.425
and fifty-seven processes were running.

55
00:02:15.425 --> 00:02:17.260
One app is often several:

56
00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.069
a browser, VS Code, Ollama.

57
00:02:20.069 --> 00:02:21.869
Here's where the kitchen breaks.

58
00:02:21.869 --> 00:02:23.297
A cook has judgement;

59
00:02:23.297 --> 00:02:25.839
a process follows its card exactly.

60
00:02:25.839 --> 00:02:28.150
Cooks really work side by side.

61
00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:30.576
A computer runs only as many processes

62
00:02:30.576 --> 00:02:32.887
at once as it has processor cores,

63
00:02:32.887 --> 00:02:35.569
and switches between the rest very fast.

64
00:02:35.569 --> 00:02:38.251
And a cook can glance at a neighbour's counter.

65
00:02:38.251 --> 00:02:41.421
A process can't, unless the system lets it in,

66
00:02:41.421 --> 00:02:43.175
the way a debugger is.

67
00:02:43.624 --> 00:02:45.760
Some cooks you never sent in.

68
00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:47.838
A service is a process the operating

69
00:02:47.838 --> 00:02:49.870
system's own manager starts,

70
00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:52.262
often at boot, with no window.

71
00:02:52.262 --> 00:02:54.340
It can run with nobody signed in,

72
00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.254
and the manager can restart it if it stops.

73
00:02:57.254 --> 00:03:00.540
On Windows, many services share one host program,

74
00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:02.920
shown as Service Host.

75
00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:04.244
On the course laptop,

76
00:03:04.244 --> 00:03:06.833
a hundred and sixty-six services were running;

77
00:03:06.833 --> 00:03:08.412
ninety-seven Service Host

78
00:03:08.412 --> 00:03:10.536
processes carried many of them.

79
00:03:10.536 --> 00:03:11.836
On a Mac or Linux,

80
00:03:11.836 --> 00:03:14.344
the same idea is called a daemon.

81
00:03:14.344 --> 00:03:17.235
In between sits the tray app: it arrives

82
00:03:17.235 --> 00:03:19.917
when you sign in and leaves when you sign out.

83
00:03:19.917 --> 00:03:22.912
On Windows and Mac, Ollama is one of those,

84
00:03:22.912 --> 00:03:24.433
not a service.

85
00:03:24.433 --> 00:03:27.080
Three kinds of cook: apps you open,

86
00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:29.182
tray apps that clock in with you,

87
00:03:29.182 --> 00:03:31.761
and night staff the building hires.

88
00:03:32.211 --> 00:03:34.568
Every kitchen keeps a staff board.

89
00:03:34.568 --> 00:03:36.948
On Windows, it's Task Manager:

90
00:03:36.948 --> 00:03:39.676
Control, Shift, Escape.

91
00:03:39.676 --> 00:03:40.768
Five years ago,

92
00:03:40.768 --> 00:03:43.229
it was mostly for ending a frozen app.

93
00:03:43.229 --> 00:03:45.713
The Processes tab groups every cook:

94
00:03:45.713 --> 00:03:49.614
apps, background processes, Windows processes.

95
00:03:49.614 --> 00:03:54.177
Details gives one row per process, with its PID.

96
00:03:54.177 --> 00:03:56.452
Services lists the night staff;

97
00:03:56.452 --> 00:03:59.831
Startup apps, who clocks in when you sign in.

98
00:03:59.831 --> 00:04:02.559
End task stops a process at once.

99
00:04:02.559 --> 00:04:05.357
Whatever it hadn't saved is gone.

100
00:04:05.357 --> 00:04:07.598
Newer Windows 11 builds add two

101
00:04:07.598 --> 00:04:09.699
hidden columns you can switch on:

102
00:04:09.699 --> 00:04:13.542
NPU, which process uses the AI chip,

103
00:04:13.542 --> 00:04:16.967
and Isolation, for apps running fenced in.

104
00:04:16.967 --> 00:04:20.044
On a Mac, the board is Activity Monitor;

105
00:04:20.044 --> 00:04:23.273
on Linux, top or htop.

106
00:04:23.724 --> 00:04:26.882
A window is a door to the cook, not the cook.

107
00:04:26.882 --> 00:04:27.706
Close the door,

108
00:04:27.706 --> 00:04:30.109
and the cook can still be at the counter.

109
00:04:30.109 --> 00:04:32.037
Three kinds keep working:

110
00:04:32.037 --> 00:04:33.906
tray apps like Ollama;

111
00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:36.332
services, which never had a door;

112
00:04:36.332 --> 00:04:38.933
and children whose parent went away.

113
00:04:38.933 --> 00:04:41.034
On Windows, ending a parent doesn't

114
00:04:41.034 --> 00:04:43.217
automatically end what it started.

115
00:04:43.217 --> 00:04:45.690
Tools that want to stop everything they launched

116
00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:48.116
have to group those processes first.

117
00:04:48.116 --> 00:04:49.312
Late last year,

118
00:04:49.312 --> 00:04:51.866
a Windows update briefly made Task Manager

119
00:04:51.866 --> 00:04:54.211
itself keep running after you closed it.

120
00:04:54.211 --> 00:04:56.721
Microsoft fixed it in November.

121
00:04:57.171 --> 00:04:59.470
A server is a process that stays running

122
00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:01.583
and waits at a numbered serving hatch,

123
00:05:01.583 --> 00:05:03.696
called a port, for orders.

124
00:05:03.696 --> 00:05:06.819
Only one process can hold a port at a time.

125
00:05:06.819 --> 00:05:08.874
Numbers you'll often see:

126
00:05:08.874 --> 00:05:14.458
3000 for many Node apps, 5173 for Vite,

127
00:05:14.458 --> 00:05:19.125
8000 for Python, 8188 for ComfyUI,

128
00:05:19.125 --> 00:05:25.278
1234 for LM Studio, and 11434 for Ollama.

129
00:05:25.278 --> 00:05:27.718
Localhost means your own machine.

130
00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:29.515
On the course laptop,

131
00:05:29.515 --> 00:05:32.489
twenty-seven hatches were open at once.

132
00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:38.234
Node says: address already in use, port 3000.

133
00:05:38.234 --> 00:05:40.231
A cook is already at that hatch,

134
00:05:40.231 --> 00:05:43.008
usually an earlier copy you thought you stopped.

135
00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:45.641
Vite's version is quieter.

136
00:05:45.641 --> 00:05:48.938
It says port 5173 is in use,

137
00:05:48.938 --> 00:05:53.072
trying another one, and opens 5174.

138
00:05:53.072 --> 00:05:55.126
Now two copies run,

139
00:05:55.126 --> 00:05:57.425
and your browser may still show the old one.

140
00:05:57.425 --> 00:06:00.862
Start a second Ollama, and it can't take its port.

141
00:06:00.862 --> 00:06:03.347
People call that leftover a zombie.

142
00:06:03.347 --> 00:06:06.063
Strictly, a zombie has already finished:

143
00:06:06.063 --> 00:06:07.793
just an entry the system keeps

144
00:06:07.793 --> 00:06:10.522
until its parent collects it, using nothing.

145
00:06:10.522 --> 00:06:12.739
The thing holding your port is alive.

146
00:06:12.739 --> 00:06:16.897
It's a leftover, or orphan, whose parent is gone.

147
00:06:17.347 --> 00:06:18.485
Five years ago,

148
00:06:18.485 --> 00:06:21.712
a builder ran maybe one dev server,

149
00:06:21.712 --> 00:06:24.464
in a terminal they opened themselves.

150
00:06:24.464 --> 00:06:27.274
Now AI tools start their own.

151
00:06:27.274 --> 00:06:29.735
A local model server is just a long-running

152
00:06:29.735 --> 00:06:32.138
process on a localhost port.

153
00:06:32.138 --> 00:06:34.692
On the course laptop, Ollama's tray app

154
00:06:34.692 --> 00:06:39.092
is the parent of the Ollama server, at port 11434.

155
00:06:39.092 --> 00:06:42.331
One app, two programs, two processes.

156
00:06:42.331 --> 00:06:44.340
By default it keeps the model loaded

157
00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:47.092
for about five minutes after your last question,

158
00:06:47.092 --> 00:06:49.367
so that memory stays taken.

159
00:06:49.367 --> 00:06:52.490
LM Studio and Microsoft's Foundry Local

160
00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:55.114
run windowless servers too.

161
00:06:55.114 --> 00:06:58.168
Ollama's server has no password by default:

162
00:06:58.168 --> 00:07:01.732
fine on localhost, risky opened to the network,

163
00:07:01.732 --> 00:07:05.344
and researchers have found many exposed online.

164
00:07:05.794 --> 00:07:09.823
When your AI app opens, it hires a crew.

165
00:07:09.823 --> 00:07:12.597
Each local MCP server in its config

166
00:07:12.597 --> 00:07:15.175
is a child process the app launches.

167
00:07:15.175 --> 00:07:17.752
Five servers, five extra cooks,

168
00:07:17.752 --> 00:07:19.714
often node or python.

169
00:07:19.714 --> 00:07:21.885
They get the satchel the app packs,

170
00:07:21.885 --> 00:07:23.534
often with keys in the config,

171
00:07:23.534 --> 00:07:25.577
and run with your permissions.

172
00:07:25.577 --> 00:07:28.363
When the app closes, it's meant to stop them,

173
00:07:28.363 --> 00:07:30.151
politely, then by force,

174
00:07:30.151 --> 00:07:33.170
and it should restart one that crashes.

175
00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:35.770
A remote one is somebody else's cook,

176
00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:37.814
in somebody else's kitchen.

177
00:07:37.814 --> 00:07:40.147
This year, researchers stressed

178
00:07:40.147 --> 00:07:43.073
that a local MCP config line is,

179
00:07:43.073 --> 00:07:45.395
in effect, a command to run.

180
00:07:45.395 --> 00:07:47.334
The makers call that by design;

181
00:07:47.334 --> 00:07:49.470
their docs advise caution.

182
00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:52.862
Treat adding one like installing software.

183
00:07:53.313 --> 00:07:56.494
A coding agent is a head cook sending in helpers.

184
00:07:56.494 --> 00:07:59.420
Every command it runs is its own child process,

185
00:07:59.420 --> 00:08:02.531
so a dev server it starts belongs to the agent.

186
00:08:02.531 --> 00:08:05.248
Long commands go to the background.

187
00:08:05.248 --> 00:08:08.545
In Claude Code, /tasks lists them,

188
00:08:08.545 --> 00:08:11.111
and it says it cleans them up when it exits.

189
00:08:11.111 --> 00:08:12.701
Not every tool does.

190
00:08:12.701 --> 00:08:14.083
What's new is fences:

191
00:08:14.083 --> 00:08:16.173
sandboxes limiting what its commands

192
00:08:16.173 --> 00:08:18.112
and their children can touch.

193
00:08:18.112 --> 00:08:20.945
As of this month, Claude Code's sandbox

194
00:08:20.945 --> 00:08:25.948
covers Mac, Linux and WSL2, not native Windows.

195
00:08:25.948 --> 00:08:28.410
Codex's Windows sandbox also stops

196
00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:30.558
leftover child processes.

197
00:08:30.558 --> 00:08:33.170
And Microsoft's Execution Containers,

198
00:08:33.170 --> 00:08:35.666
a way to fence agents in on Windows,

199
00:08:35.666 --> 00:08:38.093
became generally available this month.

200
00:08:38.093 --> 00:08:40.113
Some agents use them already;

201
00:08:40.113 --> 00:08:41.866
others say they will.

202
00:08:41.866 --> 00:08:44.362
Task Manager's Isolation column shows only

203
00:08:44.362 --> 00:08:47.160
the fences built on Windows' app containers.

204
00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:49.412
Vendors are moving toward agents as their own

205
00:08:49.412 --> 00:08:52.595
kind of process, with their own account.

206
00:08:53.045 --> 00:08:56.145
Some of the night staff are already AI.

207
00:08:56.145 --> 00:08:58.455
On the course laptop, the services list

208
00:08:58.455 --> 00:09:02.228
included Windows AI Components Host, a Codex

209
00:09:02.228 --> 00:09:06.129
sandbox service shown under the name ChatGPT,

210
00:09:06.129 --> 00:09:08.985
and a Copilot elevation service.

211
00:09:08.985 --> 00:09:10.645
Chrome can download its own

212
00:09:10.645 --> 00:09:13.014
on-device model in the background;

213
00:09:13.014 --> 00:09:16.137
newer versions have a switch to turn it off.

214
00:09:16.137 --> 00:09:18.993
Taking stock of them, and what each can see,

215
00:09:18.993 --> 00:09:22.187
is module one, lesson twelve.

216
00:09:22.638 --> 00:09:25.599
The agent can find a process and stop it.

217
00:09:25.599 --> 00:09:27.897
Your job is to judge what should be running,

218
00:09:27.897 --> 00:09:29.488
with five questions.

219
00:09:29.488 --> 00:09:31.868
Is it a file, a running copy,

220
00:09:31.868 --> 00:09:34.921
or a service that comes back after a restart?

221
00:09:34.921 --> 00:09:37.963
Who started it: you, a terminal,

222
00:09:37.963 --> 00:09:41.028
an agent, a tray app, or the system?

223
00:09:41.028 --> 00:09:42.375
Which port does it hold?

224
00:09:42.375 --> 00:09:44.116
Does it carry your keys?

225
00:09:44.116 --> 00:09:45.892
And is it fenced in, or running

226
00:09:45.892 --> 00:09:47.622
with your full access?

227
00:09:47.622 --> 00:09:48.957
Whoever started it,

228
00:09:48.957 --> 00:09:52.336
the system's board shows nearly every process;

229
00:09:52.336 --> 00:09:56.843
WSL and virtual machines show as one row.

230
00:09:57.293 --> 00:09:59.220
Safe to ignore for now:

231
00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:00.695
threads and priorities,

232
00:10:00.695 --> 00:10:02.970
what each Service Host row does,

233
00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:04.735
and Efficiency mode.

234
00:10:04.735 --> 00:10:09.205
Don't memorise PIDs; they change every run.

235
00:10:09.205 --> 00:10:12.597
Power tools like Process Explorer can wait.

236
00:10:13.046 --> 00:10:16.041
Keep this. A program is a recipe;

237
00:10:16.041 --> 00:10:18.317
a process is a cook using it.

238
00:10:18.317 --> 00:10:20.337
One file can run many times,

239
00:10:20.337 --> 00:10:23.495
each with its own number, memory and settings.

240
00:10:23.495 --> 00:10:25.469
A service is a process the system

241
00:10:25.469 --> 00:10:27.686
keeps alive with no window.

242
00:10:27.686 --> 00:10:30.101
Closing a window doesn't end a process,

243
00:10:30.101 --> 00:10:32.214
and "port already in use" usually

244
00:10:32.214 --> 00:10:34.733
means an old copy holds the hatch.

245
00:10:34.733 --> 00:10:37.322
Your AI tools are processes too.

246
00:10:37.322 --> 00:10:39.969
They run as you, with your keys.

247
00:10:39.969 --> 00:10:43.210
Know what should run, and whether it's fenced in.
